These statements are executed in a fresh scope:
const arr = [600, 6];
const copy = [...arr, 600];
console.log(copy.length);
From this trace, what length does the copy have?

In a short code review, a student presents the trace below. Evaluate from top to bottom:
function collect(first, ...rest) { return rest.length; }
console.log(collect(601, 2, 3, 4));
In this run, what does the call print?

A canned example demonstrates this language rule:
console.log(typeof helper);
function helper(x) { return x + 602; }
What is logged before the declaration?

Read the program below and determine its output:
function makeCounter() {
  let n = 603;
  return () => ++n;
}
const next = makeCounter();
next();
console.log(next());
On this input, what value is logged by the second call?

The team wants to know what this code logs:
const obj = { v: 604, get() { return this.v; } };
const fn = obj.get;
console.log(fn.call(obj));
